## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to frame a sign-assignment problem as a DP and implement both memoization and tabulation, a common interview pattern.

## Key Concepts
- DP state reduction to subset sum
- Space optimization with a 1D array
- Memoization vs bottom-up trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
// Tabulation and Memo versions
function countTargetSumTab(nums, S) {
  const total = nums.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0);
  if (Math.abs(S) > total || (total+S)%2 !== 0) return 0;
  const target = (total+S)/2;
  const dp = Array(target+1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;
  for (const n of nums) {
    for (let t = target; t >= n; t--) dp[t] += dp[t-n];
  }
  return dp[target];
}

function countTargetSumMemo(nums, S) {
  const total = nums.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0);
  if (Math.abs(S) > total || (total+S)%2 !== 0) return 0;
  const target = (total+S)/2;
  const memo = new Map();
  function dfs(i, sum){
    if (sum > target) return 0;
    if (i === nums.length) return sum === target ? 1 : 0;
    const key = i+':'+sum;
    if (memo.has(key)) return memo.get(key);
    const res = dfs(i+1, sum+nums[i]) + dfs(i+1, sum);
    memo.set(key, res);
    return res;
  }
  return dfs(0,0);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to combine shortest-path computation, path counting, and path reconstruction into a cohesive solution. It also evaluates handling of multiple equal-cost routes and lexicographic tie-breaking.

## Key Concepts
- Dijkstra's algorithm for O(m log n) shortest paths
- Shortest-path DAG construction
- Topological ordering by distance values
- DP for counting distinct shortest paths
- Lexicographic path reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestStats(n, edges, s, t) {
  const adj = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  for (const [u, v, w] of edges) {
    adj[u].push([v, w]);
  }

  const dist = new Array(n).fill(Infinity);
  const pq = [[0, s]];
  dist[s] = 0;

  while (pq.length) {
    const [d, u] = pq.pop();
    if (d > dist[u]) continue;
    for (const [v, w] of adj[u]) {
      if (dist[u] + w < dist[v]) {
        dist[v] = dist[u] + w;
        pq.push([dist[v], v]);
      }
    }
  }

  const dag = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  for (let u = 0; u < n; u++) {
    for (const [v, w] of adj[u]) {
      if (dist[u] + w === dist[v]) {
        dag[u].push(v);
      }
    }
  }

  const ways = new Array(n).fill(0);
  ways[s] = 1;
  const order = Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) => i)
    .sort((a, b) => dist[a] - dist[b]);

  for (const u of order) {
    for (const v of dag[u]) {
      ways[v] = (ways[v] + ways[u]) % 1000000007;
    }
  }

  const path = [];
  let curr = t;
  while (curr !== null) {
    path.unshift(curr);
    const preds = dag
      .map((list, u) => list.includes(curr) ? u : -1)
      .filter(u => u >= 0 && dist[curr] === dist[u] + 
        adj[u].find(([v]) => v === curr)?.[1]);
    preds.sort((a, b) => a - b);
    curr = preds.length > 0 ? preds[0] : null;
    if (curr === s) {
      path.unshift(s);
      break;
    }
  }

  return { distance: dist[t], ways: ways[t], path };
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- Dijkstra: O(m log n)
- DAG construction: O(m)
- DP counting: O(m)
- Path reconstruction: O(n + m)
- Overall: O(m log n)

